{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs PyPy 2 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following PyPy 2 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nAmr bought a new video game \"Guess Your Way Out!\". The goal of the game is to find an exit from the maze that looks like a perfect binary tree of height h. The player is initially standing at the root of the tree and the exit from the tree is located at some leaf node. Let's index all the leaf nodes from the left to the right from 1 to 2h. The exit is located at some node n where 1\u2009\u2264\u2009n\u2009\u2264\u20092h, the player doesn't know where the exit is so he has to guess his way out!Amr follows simple algorithm to choose the path. Let's consider infinite command string \"LRLRLRLRL...\" (consisting of alternating characters 'L' and 'R'). Amr sequentially executes the characters of the string using following rules:  Character 'L' means \"go to the left child of the current node\";  Character 'R' means \"go to the right child of the current node\";  If the destination node is already visited, Amr skips current command, otherwise he moves to the destination node;  If Amr skipped two consecutive commands, he goes back to the parent of the current node before executing next command;  If he reached a leaf node that is not the exit, he returns to the parent of the current node;  If he reaches an exit, the game is finished. Now Amr wonders, if he follows this algorithm, how many nodes he is going to visit before reaching the exit?\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the PyPy 2 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nh,n=map(int,raw_input().split())\nBT=[0]*(2**(h+1)-1)\nBT[2**(h+1)-2-(2**h-n)]= 'E'\nt=0\nc=0\nm='L'\ns=0\nwhile True:\n\tif s==2:\n\t\tc=(c-1)//2\n\t\tif BT[c]=='E': break\n\t\ts=0\n\telif m=='L':\n\t\tif 2*c+1>=len(BT):\n\t\t\tc=(c-1)//2\n\t\telif BT[2*c+1]=='E': break\n\t\telif BT[2*c+1]==0:\n\t\t\tc=2*c+1\n\t\t\tBT[c]=1\n\t\telse: s+=1\n\t\tm='R'\n\telif m=='R':\n\t\tif 2*c+2>=len(BT):\n\t\t\tc=(c-1)//2\n\t\telif BT[2*c+2]=='E': break\n\t\telif BT[2*c+2]==0:\n\t\t\tc=2*c+2\n\t\t\tBT[c]=1\n\t\telse: s+=1\n\t\tm='L'\nprint sum(1 for x in BT if x==1)+1\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nh, n = map(int, raw_input().split())\n\n# We'll simulate the traversal using the structure of a perfect binary tree\n# Instead of building the whole tree, we can use bit manipulation and tree properties\n\n# The exit leaf is at position n (1-indexed from left)\n# In a perfect binary tree of height h, leaves are at depth h (0-indexed depth of root is 0)\n# We can convert n to binary to determine the path from root to exit\n\n# Convert n-1 to binary (0-indexed) with h bits\n# Left = 0, Right = 1\npath = []\ntemp = n - 1\nfor i in range(h):\n    path.append(temp % 2)\n    temp //= 2\npath.reverse()\n\n# Now simulate the algorithm\n# We need to count visited nodes before reaching exit\nvisited = set()\ncurrent = 0  # root node index (using 0-based indexing for a complete binary tree)\nvisited.add(current)\n\n# The command string is \"LRLRLRLR...\" alternating starting with 'L'\ncommand_index = 0\nskipped_consecutive = 0\ntotal_visited = 1  # starting at root\n\n# We'll traverse until we reach the exit\nwhile True:\n    # Determine current command\n    cmd = 'L' if command_index % 2 == 0 else 'R'\n    \n    # Calculate child node\n    if cmd == 'L':\n        child = 2 * current + 1\n    else:\n        child = 2 * current + 2\n    \n    # Check if child is valid (within tree bounds)\n    if child >= (2 ** h):  # beyond leaf level\n        # Go to parent\n        current = (current - 1) // 2\n        skipped_consecutive += 1\n    elif child in visited:\n        # Already visited, skip\n        skipped_consecutive += 1\n    else:\n        # Move to child\n        current = child\n        visited.add(current)\n        total_visited += 1\n        skipped_consecutive = 0\n        \n        # Check if it's a leaf\n        if current >= (2 ** h - 1):  # leaf node\n            # Check if it's the exit\n            # Convert current node index to its leaf position\n            # For a perfect binary tree, leaves start at index 2^(h-1) - 1 + 1 = 2^(h-1)\n            # Actually, in 0-based indexing, leaves are from 2^(h-1) - 1 to 2^h - 2\n            # But we need to map the path to the leaf position\n            \n            # Reconstruct the path to this leaf\n            leaf_pos = 0\n            node = current\n            depth = 0\n            temp_path = []\n            while node > 0:\n                parent = (node - 1) // 2\n                if node == 2 * parent + 1:\n                    temp_path.append(0)  # left\n                else:\n                    temp_path.append(1)  # right\n                node = parent\n                depth += 1\n            \n            # Pad to h levels\n            while len(temp_path) < h:\n                temp_path.append(0)\n            temp_path.reverse()\n            \n            # Calculate leaf position (1-indexed)\n            pos = 0\n            for bit in temp_path:\n                pos = pos * 2 + bit\n            pos += 1\n            \n            if pos == n:\n                break\n            else:\n                # Return to parent (backtrack)\n                current = (current - 1) // 2\n        elif current == (2 ** h - 2):  # last possible node before leaves\n            # This shouldn't happen in a perfect tree, but just in case\n            current = (current - 1) // 2\n    \n    # Check for two consecutive skips\n    if skipped_consecutive == 2:\n        current = (current - 1) // 2\n        skipped_consecutive = 0\n    \n    # Check if we reached exit at root (shouldn't happen unless h=0)\n    if current == 0 and h == 0:\n        break\n    \n    command_index += 1\n\nprint total_visited\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-1.311301275563892e-06, -0.00013612773909699172, -1.311301275563892e-06, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -0.16343171894550323, -0.6326947212219238, -1.1801649634435307e-05, -1.8715683836489916e-05, -5.400034933700226e-05, -2.7894584491150454e-05, 0.0, -0.0001481661747675389, -4.768370445162873e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -0.11283203959465027, -0.01980377733707428, -0.6709296107292175, -0.7705033421516418, -0.01322521548718214, -4.649054244509898e-05, -0.5857030749320984, -0.7871964573860168, -0.41654929518699646, -1.0260242223739624, -3.313963316031732e-05, -0.6367547512054443, -0.005480977240949869, -4.0649541915627196e-05, -3.6954811548639555e-06, -0.026597192510962486, -3.099436753473128e-06, -0.13299347460269928, -2.145764938177308e-06, -0.6616656184196472, -0.3066173493862152, -0.388784795999527, -8.11782301752828e-05, -0.04089439660310745, -0.00013565097469836473, -0.251116544008255, -0.9889802932739258, -0.2887845039367676, -0.05531981587409973, -0.7616709470748901, -1.0856547355651855, -0.003871447406709194, -0.012689677998423576, -0.029363274574279785, -0.35408544540405273, -0.43317610025405884, -0.1993170529603958, -0.013531040400266647, -0.01890544779598713, -0.0016563518438488245, -0.008626